    "quality_findings": [
      "Normal RGBA composites on gray/light backgrounds show substantial reduction of muddy paint, soft grey fringe and smeared clothing detail across all three attack views. This is visible compared with the actual v12 cells, unlike the tested restoration-only candidates.",
      "Eyes, fringe, cheek/jaw boundary, clasps, belt and straight sword are more legible. Front/Side faces do not become narrower during the cleanup.",
      "The existing low-to-high draw, extended elbow/shoulder follow-through, returning arm and anatomical right-sword/left-scabbard roles remain readable without detached joints or new wrist flips.",
      "Dark red slash effects remain attached to the sword trajectory and fully inside their whole registered cells."
    ],
    "geometry_findings": {
      "front": "Frame0-2 skull78 versus Idle75, boots54 versus53. Frame14-15 skull80 retains a wider upper hair silhouette; eyes/cheeks/torso/boots do not expand in lockstep. End boots54-55. Accepted as small hair/detail differences rather than hidden whole-model inflation. Frame13 exposes a slightly longer blade, in the same returning direction as the prior art.",
      "side": "First/end neutral skull83-84 versus Idle82, boots56-57 versus55 after whole last-batch framing correction. Face readability improves and no renewed narrow-face drift is seen.",
      "back": "Initial oversized48px boots were rejected and source corrected. Final neutral boots46 atstart and44 atend versus Idle44. Upper-hair neutral width78-80 is somewhat wider than Idle74 but close to preceding attack76-79. Frame7-to8 crown lowers~5.6nativepx as head/upper body lean left/forward into the upswing; boot source width87->89 does not shrink, so this is not uniform whole-character shrink. It is retained rather than erased by per-frame scaling."
    },
